  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A powerful coastal storm will continue to pull \naway from the region today with improving conditions. \n\nStrong low pressure continues to slowly slide northward away from \nthe region. Weak high pressure will very briefly settle in to the \nregion through tonight. Clouds will dwindle today into tonight,\nwith dry conditions expected. Temperatures will be near to \nslightly above normal today and tonight, with highs in the low-\nmid 50s and lows in the low to mid 30s. It will remain breezy \nhowever, with winds gusting as high as 30 mph this afternoon.\nWinds will dwindle notably tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread rain and windy conditions is \nexpected across our region with the next cold front late Sunday \ninto Monday. \n\nA new area of low pressure will be lifting north and west of \nthe region Sunday night through Monday. This is a very different\ntrack than the track that yesterday's system took. The main \nimpacts we are watching for this system are widespread rain \nparticularly Sunday Night, and windy conditions on Monday.\n\nFor the heavy rain concern, most model guidance shows rain \namounts in the 0.5 to 1 inch range, which is low enough to limit\nany flash flood threat at this point. In addition, the timing \nof the rain overnight, should limit instability, thus decreasing\nthe risk for localized downpours.\n\nOne new wrinkle is a hint of instability in the guidance. Latest\nNBM probabilities have inserted a slight chance of thunder in\nparts of New Jersey for Sunday night, and hi-res guidance hints\nthat some sort of low-topped squall line may try to develop\nalong the cold front. With limited surface instability, its\nhighly uncertain if any significant weather could occur with\nthis, but we'll be looking closer at it in the coming days.\n\nThe strongest winds are going to come behind a cold front \nduring the day on Monday. There is less agreement between the \nmodels on wind speeds (as compared to rain amounts), but at this\npoint, it looks as if peak gusts of up to 45 mph will be \npossible over portions of the region. If this trend continues, a\nwind advisory may be needed particularly for the higher terrain\nof the southern Poconos and NW NJ, and along the coastal areas.",
